## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of probabilistic scheduling, fairness, and simple kernel-like data paths without overwhelming complexity.

## Key Concepts
- Lottery scheduling basics; per-process tickets; cumulative distribution; random number generation; blocking/wake interactions.
- Data structures: ready list with ticket counts; total_tickets; small RNG; race-free updates.
- Testing: compare empirical waiting time and starvation under IO-bound vs CPU-bound mixes.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int pid;
  int tickets;
  int state; // READY/BLOCKED
} proc_t;

static uint32_t rng = 12345;
static uint32_t lcg(void){ rng = 1664525u*rng + 1013904223u; return rng; }

proc_t* select_next(proc_t** procs, int n){
  int total = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY) total += procs[i]->tickets;
  if(total<=0) return NULL;
  int r = lcg() % total + 1;
  int acc = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY){ acc += procs[i]->tickets; if(acc >= r) return procs[i]; }
  return NULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple CPUs and true parallel dispatch?
- How would you prevent ticket manipulation by a misbehaving process and ensure numerical robustness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of multi-tenant scheduling, latency guarantees, and dynamic resource control under pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Latency-aware scheduling across containers
- Per-container quotas and dynamic weights
- Runnable vs blocked time accounting
- Preemption policy and fairness under contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int id;
  uint64_t runnable_ns;
  uint64_t blocked_ns;
  uint64_t cpu_quota_ns;
  uint64_t base_weight;
  // ... other fields
} container_t;

static inline uint64_t total_time(container_t *c){
  return c->runnable_ns + c->blocked_ns;
}

void update_weight(container_t *c){
  uint64_t t = total_time(c);
  if (t == 0) t = 1;
  c->weight = (uint64_t)(c->base_weight * c->runnable_ns / t);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate latency under mixed UI and batch workloads? 
- How would you adapt the policy for sudden memory pressure spikes or NUMA effects?
